From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: kebab refactor <kebab@local.invalid>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 11:37:58 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm/dpu: bail out of an endless frame-done timeout storm

dpu_encoder_frame_done_timeout() never clears frame_busy_mask and never
deletes the timer, so it can never reach its own "invalid timeout,
frame_busy_mask=0" early return. On a link that fails to train, the timer
re-arms and fires indefinitely and the encoder never reaches idle.

The consequence is not confined to DRM. Observed on sm8250 (OnePlus 8T)
with a 4K monitor attached directly over USB-C DisplayPort, where link
training fails at the physical layer:

  msm_dp_ctrl_link_train_1_2: max v_level reached
  msm_dp_ctrl_link_train_1_2: link training #1 on phy 0 failed. ret=-11
  msm_dp_bridge_atomic_enable: Failed link training (rc=-104)
  dpu_encoder_phys_vid_wait_for_commit_done: vblank timeout: 80a29300
  dpu_kms_wait_for_commit_done: wait for commit done returned -110
  dpu_encoder_frame_done_timeout: enc37 frame done timeout   (x4)

After that the compositor is left deadlocked with the modeset locks held
across an atomic commit that can never complete: reading
/sys/kernel/debug/dri/0/state blocks, and the display and touch input are
gone even though the kernel itself stays quiescent and no task is in
uninterruptible sleep. The device survives only because userspace stops
retrying.

Mirror the cleanup that dpu_encoder_frame_done_callback() already does on
the success path once a dead link is unambiguous, so the encoder can go
idle and the commit fails cleanly instead of wedging.

Four consecutive timeouts is the threshold; the storms observed on this
hardware arrive in bursts of exactly that size.
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_encoder.c | 29 +++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 29 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_encoder.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_encoder.c
index 778e231d4..7a5002e30 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_encoder.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_encoder.c
@@ -2707,6 +2707,9 @@ static int dpu_encoder_setup_display(struct dpu_encoder_virt *dpu_enc,
 	return ret;
 }
 
+/* Bound the frame-done timeout retry storm; see below. */
+#define DPU_FRAME_DONE_MAX_CONSECUTIVE_TIMEOUTS 4
+
 static void dpu_encoder_frame_done_timeout(struct timer_list *t)
 {
 	struct dpu_encoder_virt *dpu_enc = timer_container_of(dpu_enc, t,
@@ -2733,6 +2736,32 @@ static void dpu_encoder_frame_done_timeout(struct timer_list *t)
 	if (atomic_inc_return(&dpu_enc->frame_done_timeout_cnt) == 1)
 		msm_disp_snapshot_state(drm_enc->dev);
 
+	/*
+	 * Give up cleanly after repeated consecutive timeouts on a dead link
+	 * instead of leaving frame_busy_mask permanently set. Unlike the
+	 * success path in dpu_encoder_frame_done_callback(), this timeout
+	 * path never clears frame_busy_mask or deletes the timer, so it can
+	 * never reach its own "invalid timeout, frame_busy_mask=0" early
+	 * return above: a link that fails to train keeps this timer
+	 * re-arming and firing every ~83 ms indefinitely, which on real
+	 * hardware ran for 18+ seconds and then ended in a silent hard SoC
+	 * reset instead of a clean failure.
+	 */
+	if (atomic_read(&dpu_enc->frame_done_timeout_cnt) >=
+			DPU_FRAME_DONE_MAX_CONSECUTIVE_TIMEOUTS) {
+		unsigned int i;
+
+		DPU_ERROR_ENC(dpu_enc,
+			"giving up after %d consecutive frame done timeouts, forcing idle\n",
+			DPU_FRAME_DONE_MAX_CONSECUTIVE_TIMEOUTS);
+
+		for (i = 0; i < dpu_enc->num_phys_encs; i++)
+			clear_bit(i, dpu_enc->frame_busy_mask);
+
+		if (!dpu_enc->frame_busy_mask[0])
+			timer_delete(&dpu_enc->frame_done_timer);
+	}
+
 	event = DPU_ENCODER_FRAME_EVENT_ERROR;
 	trace_dpu_enc_frame_done_timeout(DRMID(drm_enc), event);
 	dpu_crtc_frame_event_cb(dpu_enc->crtc, event);
